<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/034E">Example <span data-tag="034E">4.5.7.8</span> <span class="named">(Isofibrant Towers)</span>.</a> Let $\mathscr {F}: \operatorname{\mathbf{Z}}_{\geq 0}^{\operatorname{op}} \rightarrow \operatorname{Set_{\Delta }}$ be a diagram, which we identify with a tower of simplicial sets </p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \cdots \rightarrow \mathscr {F}(3) \rightarrow \mathscr {F}(2) \rightarrow \mathscr {F}(1) \rightarrow \mathscr {F}(0). \]
</div>
<p> Then $\mathscr {F}$ is isofibrant (in the sense of Definition <a data-tag="0349" href="/tag/0349">4.5.7.3</a>) if and only if each of the simplicial sets $\mathscr {F}(n)$ is an $\infty $-category and each of the transition functors $\mathscr {F}(n+1) \rightarrow \mathscr {F}(n)$ is an isofibration of $\infty $-categories. </p>
</article>
